Relevant columns and potential usage  
• PRODUCTS."category": groups every product into a business category that we finally need to rank by purchase quantity.  
• PRODUCTS."id": primary key that lets us join PRODUCTS to ORDER_ITEMS.  
• ORDER_ITEMS."product_id": foreign key to PRODUCTS."id"; counts of these rows give us “purchase quantity”.  
• ORDER_ITEMS."status": lets us decide whether the row represents a successful purchase (“Complete”, “Shipped”, etc.).  
• EVENTS."event_type": lets us isolate page‑view events.  
• EVENTS."uri": contains the product‑page path (normally “/products/<product‑id>”), so we can tie a page view back to a product.  
• EVENTS."session_id", EVENTS."created_at": used to calculate the “time on page” → LEAD("created_at") OVER (PARTITION BY "session_id" ORDER BY "created_at").  
• EVENTS."user_id": enables optional user‑level filters if needed.

Conditions we might apply  
• ORDER_ITEMS."status" IN ('Complete', 'Shipped') to count only successful purchases.  
• EVENTS."event_type" = 'page_view' (or URI pattern) to isolate product‑page views.  
• EVENTS."uri" ILIKE '/products/%' to grab events that are for product pages.  
• JOIN PRODUCTS ON PRODUCTS."id" = ORDER_ITEMS."product_id" to bring in the category.  
• Extract product_id from EVENTS."uri" with SPLIT_PART or REGEXP if needed.  
• Calculate time‑on‑page with LEAD inside each "session_id".  
• Finally average the minutes by category.

Below are 10 exploratory Snowflake SQL queries (each limited to 20 rows) that move from very simple inspection toward the complete analytical steps.  

```sql
-- 1. Description: See a list of distinct product categories in the catalog.
SELECT DISTINCT "category"
FROM "THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."PRODUCTS"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Description: Quick count of order‑item rows per status to understand valid purchase states.
SELECT "status", COUNT(*) AS "row_cnt"
FROM "THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."ORDER_ITEMS"
GROUP BY "status"
ORDER BY "row_cnt" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Description: Join ORDER_ITEMS → PRODUCTS to view one example of (product_id, category, status).
SELECT oi."product_id",
       p."category",
       oi."status"
FROM "THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."ORDER_ITEMS" AS oi
JOIN "THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."PRODUCTS"  AS p
  ON p."id" = oi."product_id"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Description: Aggregate purchase quantity (“units”) by category, restricted to complete or shipped items.
SELECT p."category",
       COUNT(*) AS "purchase_qty"
FROM "THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."ORDER_ITEMS" AS oi
JOIN "THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."PRODUCTS"  AS p
  ON p."id" = oi."product_id"
WHERE oi."status" IN ('Complete','Shipped')
GROUP BY p."category"
ORDER BY "purchase_qty" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Description: Pick the single top‑selling category (useful in later queries).
SELECT p."category",
       COUNT(*) AS "purchase_qty"
FROM "THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."ORDER_ITEMS" AS oi
JOIN "THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."PRODUCTS"  AS p
  ON p."id" = oi."product_id"
WHERE oi."status" IN ('Complete','Shipped')
GROUP BY p."category"
ORDER BY "purchase_qty" DESC
LIMIT 1;
```

```sql
-- 6. Description: Inspect event types and how often they occur.
SELECT "event_type", COUNT(*) AS "events"
FROM "THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."EVENTS"
GROUP BY "event_type"
ORDER BY "events" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Description: Look at sample URIs that resemble product pages.
SELECT DISTINCT "uri"
FROM "THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."EVENTS"
WHERE "uri" ILIKE '/products/%'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Description: For a random sample of product‑page views, extract the numeric product_id
--                 and check if the product is in the previously found top category.
SELECT e."session_id",
       e."created_at",
       e."uri",
       TRY_TO_NUMBER(SPLIT_PART(e."uri", '/', 3)) AS "product_id"
FROM "THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."EVENTS" AS e
WHERE e."uri" ILIKE '/products/%'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Description: Using LEAD to calculate “time on page” in seconds for product views
--                 (example calculation over a small sample).
SELECT e."session_id",
       e."created_at"      AS "page_view_time",
       LEAD(e."created_at") OVER (PARTITION BY e."session_id" ORDER BY e."created_at") 
            AS "next_event_time",
       ( LEAD(e."created_at") OVER (PARTITION BY e."session_id" ORDER BY e."created_at")
         - e."created_at" ) / 1000000.0 AS "seconds_on_page"
FROM "THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."EVENTS" AS e
WHERE e."uri" ILIKE '/products/%'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. Description: End‑to‑end: average minutes on product pages **for the top‑selling category**.
--   1) CTE top_cat gets that category.  
--   2) CTE prod_ids collects all product IDs in that category.  
--   3) CTE pv gets page‑view events for those products and calculates duration.  
--   4) outer SELECT converts seconds to minutes and averages.  
WITH top_cat AS (
    SELECT p."category"
    FROM "THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."ORDER_ITEMS" AS oi
    JOIN "THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."PRODUCTS"  AS p
      ON p."id" = oi."product_id"
    WHERE oi."status" IN ('Complete','Shipped')
    GROUP BY p."category"
    ORDER BY COUNT(*) DESC
    LIMIT 1
),
prod_ids AS (
    SELECT "id" AS "product_id"
    FROM "THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."PRODUCTS"
    WHERE "category" = (SELECT "category" FROM top_cat)
),
pv AS (
    SELECT e."session_id",
           TRY_TO_NUMBER(SPLIT_PART(e."uri", '/', 3)) AS "product_id",
           e."created_at",
           LEAD(e."created_at") OVER (PARTITION BY e."session_id" ORDER BY e."created_at") 
                AS "next_event_time"
    FROM "THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."EVENTS" AS e
    WHERE e."uri" ILIKE '/products/%'
)
SELECT AVG( ("next_event_time" - "created_at") / 1000000.0 / 60.0 ) AS "avg_minutes_on_product_page"
FROM pv
WHERE "product_id" IN (SELECT "product_id" FROM prod_ids)
      AND "next_event_time" IS NOT NULL
LIMIT 20;
```

